WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.079
Recently Australia got to see a total lunar eclipse

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:11.429
This is when the earth gets between the Sun and the moon and the shadow of the earth is cast on the moon

00:00:11.679 --> 00:00:13.170
Turning it dark and red

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:20.189
I was able to go and see the lunar eclipse with some of my friends and it actually got me thinking about the topic of being

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:24.509
An expert on something or at least being perceived as an expert

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:30.059
So back in I guess a previous life of mine when I was an undergrad in New Zealand

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.640
I was involved in an urban astronomy group

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.520
we would take telescopes out onto the street and

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:42.239
I would show members of the public through the telescope and answer their questions about space and about astronomy

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:48.419
I guess during that time I would have been expected to be an expert on things like a lunar eclipse

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.410
phases of the Moon and how all of that is working and

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.199
Being in that role gave me a lot of confidence to answer questions on that topic

00:00:58.359 --> 00:01:06.088
even if I didn't totally understand them I felt a level of confidence to i guess re-say

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.220
Explanations that I'd heard other people give or to say something that I knew made physical sense

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:15.779
Enough so that it would satisfy me and satisfy the person asking the question

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:22.169
Even if I did have some of my deeper like questions myself about how this was working

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:27.509
I wouldn't really raise them because I was expected to be in the role of answering the questions not asking them

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.559
when I went up to watch the Eclipse the other week, I

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:35.879
Didn't really tell these friends I was with about that

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:42.030
you know experience that I had with telescopes and stuff and I just sort of took a backseat role of

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.769
Letting someone else in the group stand up as that expert and answer our questions

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.979
about how the Eclipse was working because I still had things I didn't totally understand and that I wanted to know more about

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.740
So I found viewing the Eclipse to be really fun because I was able to ask questions

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:05.080
Things like you know, how much darker is the moon going to get and how long is it going to last?

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.560
And which countries can see it right now?

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:15.299
and I don't think I would have felt confident asking those questions if I had been in the role of the expected expert I

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.670
Think it was only

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:19.530
Yeah from that backseat role

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:26.489
I was able to really engage my own curiosity instead of standing on I guess a pedestal and just

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.869
Regurgitating explanations that I had sort of heard or read on the internet or something

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.930
And I don't know when this sort of thing

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:42.239
started but it's definitely something I've faced a lot in my life as being that expected expert in a topic like

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:50.370
way back in high school people who knew in my class and my teachers that I had quite an interest in physics and astronomy and

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:56.789
Even back then like the teachers would maybe pick on me to answer a question about some obscure astronomy thing

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.459
And I probably wouldn't know the answer, but I would feel like I had to like I was responsible for knowing

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:05.159
All the physics and astronomy at that age

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.869
Even though that's quite absurd for anyone to ever know all of the subject

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.370
I would feel that just because my peers and my class expected me to be

00:03:14.889 --> 00:03:22.139
toby the person that knows stuff about physics and astronomy that if I didn't know the answer it was sort of shameful and that I

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:29.910
didn't really feel comfortable as much as I should have asking other people in my class questions about physics because I

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.880
was the one who was expected to be the expert on this and not them and it's quite a dangerous mindset to

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:44.129
stop your own questions and your own curiosity because you are suddenly expected to know more about it than other people

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.829
Like even going on through uni, and then I graduated with a physics degree

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:57.179
It still sort of hampers me in a way because when I'm with people I'm afraid that if I ask too many

00:03:57.579 --> 00:04:03.299
Perceived dumb questions about physics. They will look at me and think like, how did you get your physics degree?

00:04:03.299 --> 00:04:07.888
like as if I have a lack of confidence in the worthiness of my

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:13.469
Physics work and of my physics degree that if I ask too many dumb questions, I didn't deserve it

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.880
And like I said, I think it's quite damaging to be in that role

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.499
Like I do have the degree, which means I do know some stuff about physics

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.239
but I still think it's absurd for anyone to think that they know all of physics and

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:31.560
There are going to be mistakes that I make in explanations and things I don't understand

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:39.779
But yeah, I feel like I need to gain more confidence in asking the questions despite my qualifications

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:46.500
I think someone with the qualification who holds that up as meaning that you should trust them and everything

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:52.529
They say is a bad thing because your qualification shouldn't really give you so much

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:58.200
Superiority over other people. It should represent that you have at least proven

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:04.979
You know a base level of things but I don't think there's anything that really proves that you should trust every word

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:10.919
Someone says I think you should always  question people with qualifications and people with qualifications

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.530
should ask questions of

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.510
everyone else. I get how saying this could also be damaging in the other direction like

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.619
you want people who do have levels of

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:27.899
Understanding to sometimes be the ones to stand up and tell people how something works

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.340
and you want people to believe in scientists and believe in people who have

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:39.300
Gone through like all the education system and do actually know what they're talking about

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:46.740
because a lot of problems in science come when people deny facts that scientists try to tell them and there's this whole

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.989
mistrust in science going on

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:51.539
so it can be damaging to say like

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:58.709
People with qualifications shouldn't act like they know everything because sometimes that confidence is what they need to do

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.410
to get important ideas across

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:07.589
But I don't really know the answer here like I think it's a bit of both if you're going to have

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.250
people

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:16.019
Without qualifications and without the education standing up and being the ones who are confident enough to say listen to me

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:20.770
This is how something works and you've got all the scientists standing on the other hand saying

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:26.109
You know, we we're not quite sure in the science and we're still learning as well

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.550
We make mistakes and admitting the truth and the honesty behind science

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:37.240
Then that's going to lead to a really skewed reality where I guess the wrong people come into power

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:41.019
so I don't think scientists should be totally like

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:47.830
unconfident in what they're trying to get across but I think that scientists and people with any kind of

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.060
Interest or you know perceived expert level in something

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:58.180
Should continue asking questions and should continue engaging with people and not let themselves

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.639
think that they are on this pedestal where

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:05.169
They are only there to answer questions and not to ask them as well

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.870
I'd love to hear your thoughts on this because it's not really something I've heard people talking about before but it's just something that

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.150
Crossed my mind the other week

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.389
When I felt like for the first time in a while that I could really just ask questions without being judged

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:24.310
So leave a comment below if you've ever felt a similar thing to this and I'll see you next time

